    "acquired_at": "2026-11-20T14:29:52.260680+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-20T14:29:52.260680+00:00",
    "body": "Warehouse automation is trending — and rightfully so. Robotics and software systems solve many operational bottlenecks amid growing order volumes. They also mitigate the impact of chronic labor shortages .\nWhat’s Related\nDecision-makers should continue adopting digital technology . However, the shift to automation shouldn’t come at the expense of physical infrastructure . Here’s how prudent executives modernize tangible warehouse design elements for workplace safety and security .\n1. Color-coded floor markings to guide traffic\nThe United States Occupational Safety and Health Administration requires appropriate markings on permanent aisles and passageways used by material handling equipment, such as forklifts. However, the agency doesn’t specify how wide the lines should be or what colors to use.\nFollowing industry standards is a safe bet. For example, ANSI Z535.1 recommends orange for warning labels and yellow to communicate caution. This standard is a handy guide when color-coding aisles, traffic lanes, general walkways, equipment locations, inspection areas and other key zones across the warehouse floor.\nRELATED: How to Set Up Warehouse Automation Without the Big Price Tag\n2. Mirrors to improve visibility around blind spots\nMounting parabolic or convex mirrors makes traffic paths, loading bays and personnel exit points safer. The curvature of these mirrors expands fields of view, which can help reduce blind spots and raise situational awareness among workers.\nThese safety mirrors come in different configurations. Generally, quarter or 90-degree domes suit sharp bends. Half or 180-degree mirrors fit T-intersections, while full or 360-degree domes increase visibility in cross aisles when suspended from the ceiling.\n3. Upgraded dock doors to handle heavy daily use\nCommercial doors in warehouses and distribution centers have a demanding job. Busy loading environments require high-cycle door systems to withstand the rigors of heavy daily use. Industrial facilities often benefit from insulated sectional steel panels, which maintain consistent indoor temperature levels and protect inventory from dust and the elements.\nIntegrating a commercial door operator is key to streamlining loading and unloading operations. Models with sensors can detect obstructions and automatically reverse to prevent accidental closures.\n4. Column barriers to protect racking systems\nThese barriers absorb impact, helping keep racking posts intact when hit by forklifts, pallet jacks and transport vehicles. More warehouses have high clear heights to accommodate taller racks and support modern distribution.\nColumn barriers avert disaster, especially in facilities with autonomous mobile robots that can navigate independently across the floor and crash into racks. Too much inventory may fall if a multilevel storage structure collapses after an accidental collision. Wraparound protectors can trap debris and moisture, though, so routine inspections and periodic cleaning are essential.\n5. Bollards to safeguard entrances and traffic areas\nThese sturdy vertical posts protect commercial properties from ram-raiding. A good case in point was a ram raid at an Australian shopping center, where bollards stopped a truck used by thieves that targeted an ATM inside the mall.\nWhen properly placed, spaced and finished, bollards should be easily visible to forklift operators and freight vehicle drivers. At the same time, pedestrians should be able to pass through them with ease. Security landscaping features can complement bollards. Thoughtfully placed natural boulders, reinforced planters and concealed ditches can help keep unauthorized vehicles at bay.\nModern warehouses need strong physical infrastructure, too\nInvesting heavily in automated systems and industrial robots increases the need for better physical infrastructure. Color-coded floor markings, parabolic mirrors, insulated, high-cycle loading doors, column barriers and bollards should be high on the agenda of warehouse operations managers who value increased workplace safety and security.\nDan Parks is a senior writer at Modded.com with six years of experience specializing in supply chain content.",
